course: dictionary
lesson: 15571
title: Suspicion: a feeling that something is wrong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Suspicion is a feeling that something is wrong, even when you cannot prove it. «Mia had a suspicion that someone had taken her pencil case.» She is not sure yet, but she thinks it is probably true. Try your meaning in the sentence: does "a feeling that something is probably true" fit? Yes, so that is the meaning.
explain: Suspicion can also mean not trusting someone. «The shopkeeper watched the boy with suspicion.» The clue is watched: the shopkeeper did not fully trust him. Suspicion can also mean a very small trace of something: «There was a suspicion of a smile on her face.» Here it means only a tiny bit of a smile.
explain: In a mystery story, suspicion falls on characters, and the reader tries to find out who is guilty. «The detective put the gardener under suspicion.» Under suspicion means people think he might be guilty, but nobody has proved it yet. Look for clues like this to work out which meaning the author wants.
explain: In real life, safety matters most. If a stranger or a situation makes you feel suspicious, do not keep it to yourself. Tell a trusted adult, such as a parent, carer or teacher, straight away. Feeling suspicious is a good reason to ask for help.
fact: Suspicion is a noun: a suspicion, suspicions, or uncountable suspicion.
fact: «Suspicion» can mean a feeling that someone has done something wrong, without being sure.
fact: «A suspicion» can be a feeling or idea that something is probably true.
fact: «Suspicion» can mean not trusting someone, and «a suspicion of» something means a very small amount, like «a suspicion of a smile».
fact: Useful phrases: «under suspicion», «arouse suspicion», «on suspicion of», and «have a suspicion that».
exercise: «Mia had a suspicion that someone had taken her pencil case.» What does suspicion mean here? | A kind gift from a friend | *A feeling that something is probably true | A fast game of tag | A large, bright picture
exercise: «The shopkeeper watched the boy with suspicion.» What does suspicion mean here? | *Not trusting him | Ready to give him a prize | Falling asleep in the shop | Hungry for lunch
exercise: «The detective put the gardener under suspicion.» What does under suspicion mean? | He is the detective's best friend | He has gone home for the day | *People think he might be guilty, but it is not proved | He has won a prize for his garden
test: «Sara had a strange suspicion about the man at the gate.» What does suspicion mean here? | A plan for a birthday party | A wish to buy a new bike | *A feeling that something is probably wrong | A loud noise in the street
test: If a stranger makes you feel suspicious, what should you do? | *Tell a trusted adult, such as a parent or teacher | Follow the stranger to find out more | Keep it secret and say nothing to anyone | Ignore it, because feelings do not matter
exam: «The police arrested two men on suspicion of theft.» What does on suspicion of mean? | Because it was raining hard | *Because people think they may be guilty, but it is not yet proved | Because they were very tall | Because they had won a prize at the fair
